															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/axidos" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(611736); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=611736&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609040973" alt="Axidos" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=611736)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/axidos" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(611736); return false;" >Axidos</a></span> <span class="odate time_1294381354 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">07 Jan 2011 06:22</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-966726">
									

<p>Whether it passes the <a href="/how-to-write-an-scp#toc7">locked box test</a> for Safe hinges on whether you should account for a bug crawling into the box through the keyhole, and whether the plot of soil is genuinely obtainable to lock away.</p>
<p>However containers which seal off outside matter are almost trivial to make in modern day. Such technology was available to <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francesco_Redi#Research" onclick="window.open(this.href, '_blank'); return false;">Francesco Redi</a> and <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spontaneous_generation#Scientific_method" onclick="window.open(this.href, '_blank'); return false;">Louis Pasteur</a> in 1668 and 1859 respectively, which they used to such a degree of success they were great contributions to disproving spontaneous generation.</p>
<p>That considered, so long as the soil can actually be extracted from the rest of the earth and locked away, I'm of the belief it should be classified Safe.</p>

							</div>

								
					
